{"type":"message","id":"879f5052","parentId":"0e5978d9","timestamp":"2026-06-11T21:47:57.729Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Compare 5/6 ARM at 5.75% versus 30-year fixed at 6.75%. Provide break-even conditions and borrower profiles."}],"timestamp":1781214477725}}
{"type":"message","id":"71e102b3","parentId":"879f5052","timestamp":"2026-06-11T21:48:30.554Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Below is a practical comparison of a **5/6 ARM at 5.75%** versus a **30-year fixed at 6.75%**, assuming both are 30-year amortizing mortgages and the ARM is fixed for the first 5 years, then adjusts every 6 months.\n\n## 1. Payment comparison\n\nApproximate principal-and-interest payment per **$100,000 borrowed**:\n\n| Loan Type | Initial Rate | Monthly P&I per $100k |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| 5/6 ARM | 5.75% | ~$584 |\n| 30-year fixed | 6.75% | ~$649 |\n\n**Initial savings with ARM:** about **$65/month per $100,000 borrowed**\n\nExamples:\n\n| Loan Amount | ARM Payment | Fixed Payment | ARM Monthly Savings |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| $300,000 | ~$1,751 | ~$1,946 | ~$195 |\n| $500,000 | ~$2,918 | ~$3,243 | ~$325 |\n| $750,000 | ~$4,377 | ~$4,864 | ~$487 |\n| $1,000,000 | ~$5,835 | ~$6,486 | ~$651 |\n\nOver the first 5 years, the ARM saves roughly:\n\n**$3,900 per $100,000 borrowed**\n\nSo on a **$500,000 loan**, the ARM saves about **$19,500** in payment cash flow over the initial 5-year fixed period.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Break-even framework\n\nThe ARM is better if:\n\n> **Initial ARM savings + expected refinance/sale benefits > future rate-reset risk + refinancing costs + uncertainty premium**\n\nThe fixed loan is better if:\n\n> **You value payment certainty and/or expect to hold the loan long enough that ARM reset risk outweighs the early savings**\n\n---\n\n## 3. Simple 5-year break-even\n\nBecause the ARM saves about **$65/month per $100k**, the 5-year savings are:\n\n> **$65 × 60 = $3,900 per $100k**\n\nIf refinancing or selling before the first adjustment, the ARM is attractive if transaction costs do not exceed the savings.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Loan Amount | 5-Year ARM Savings | Approx. Refi Cost Break-Even |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| $300,000 | ~$11,700 | ARM wins if refi/sale cost < this |\n| $500,000 | ~$19,500 | ARM wins if refi/sale cost < this |\n| $750,000 | ~$29,250 | ARM wins if refi/sale cost < this |\n| $1,000,000 | ~$39,000 | ARM wins if refi/sale cost < this |\n\nIf you are highly likely to sell or refinance within 5 years, the ARM has a strong case.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Rate-reset break-even after year 5\n\nAt the end of 5 years, the ARM balance is lower than the fixed loan balance because more principal is paid down under the lower-rate ARM.\n\nApproximate remaining balance per **$100,000 original loan** after 5 years:\n\n| Loan Type | Remaining Balance per $100k |\n|---|---:|\n| 5/6 ARM at 5.75% | ~$93,452 |\n| 30-year fixed at 6.75% | ~$94,297 |\n\nSo the ARM has about **$845 more principal reduction per $100k** after 5 years.\n\nBut the key risk is what the ARM adjusts to.\n\nFor the ARM payment after year 5 to equal the original fixed-loan payment, the ARM’s new rate would need to be roughly:\n\n> **About 6.95% to 7.00%**\n\nIn other words, if the ARM resets after 5 years to **below ~7%**, the ARM payment may still be comparable to or below the 30-year fixed payment.\n\nIf it resets meaningfully above 7%, the fixed loan starts looking better from a payment-stability perspective.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Approximate post-reset payment per $100k original loan\n\nAfter 5 years, assuming the ARM balance is about **$93,452 per $100k** and 25 years remain:\n\n| ARM Reset Rate | New ARM Payment | Compared with 6.75% Fixed Payment |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 5.75% | ~$588 | Still much cheaper |\n| 6.50% | ~$631 | Cheaper |\n| 7.00% | ~$661 | Slightly higher |\n| 7.50% | ~$691 | Higher |\n| 8.00% | ~$721 | Higher |\n| 8.50% | ~$752 | Much higher |\n| 9.00% | ~$784 | Much higher |\n\nThe original 30-year fixed payment is about **$649 per $100k**.\n\nSo the ARM’s rough payment break-even after year 5 is around **6.8%–7.0%**, depending on exact amortization and adjustment terms.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key ARM terms to check\n\nThe comparison depends heavily on the ARM’s caps and index. You need to know:\n\n1. **Initial adjustment cap**  \n   Example: can it jump by 2%, 5%, etc. after year 5?\n\n2. **Periodic adjustment cap**  \n   Since it adjusts every 6 months, how much can it move each adjustment?\n\n3. **Lifetime cap**  \n   Example: 5.75% start rate with a 5% lifetime cap means max rate could be 10.75%.\n\n4. **Index and margin**  \n   Commonly SOFR + margin. The future rate is usually:\n   > Index + margin\n\n5. **Refinance assumptions**  \n   Do not assume refinancing will be available or cheap. Home value, credit score, income, and lending standards matter.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Borrower profiles\n\n### The 5/6 ARM is better for borrowers who:\n\n- Expect to **sell within 5 years**\n- Expect to **refinance within 5 years**\n- Have high confidence their income or assets will improve\n- Can tolerate payment uncertainty after year 5\n- Are comfortable monitoring rates and refinancing opportunities\n- Are borrowing a large amount, where the 1% rate difference creates meaningful savings\n- Have enough liquidity to handle a higher payment if the ARM adjusts upward\n- Are buying a home they do not expect to keep long-term\n\nBest fit:  \n**Mobile professionals, high-income borrowers, people expecting a liquidity event, buyers planning to upgrade/move, or financially flexible borrowers.**\n\n---\n\n### The 30-year fixed is better for borrowers who:\n\n- Expect to stay in the home long term\n- Want payment certainty\n- Would be financially stressed by a higher payment after year 5\n- Do not want to depend on refinancing\n- Believe rates may stay high or rise\n- Have limited emergency reserves\n- Are near their maximum affordable payment already\n- Prefer simplicity and peace of mind\n\nBest fit:  \n**Long-term homeowners, families seeking stability, risk-averse borrowers, retirees, or anyone with tight debt-to-income ratios.**\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe **5/6 ARM at 5.75%** gives meaningful early savings: about **$65/month per $100,000 borrowed**, or roughly **$3,900 per $100,000 over 5 years**.\n\nIt is likely the better choice if you expect to **sell or refinance within 5 years** or if you can comfortably handle adjustment risk.\n\nThe **30-year fixed at 6.75%** is better if you want **long-term certainty**, expect to keep the mortgage well beyond 5 years, or would be hurt by the ARM resetting to **7.5%, 8%, or higher**.\n\nA useful rule of thumb:\n\n> If you are very likely out of the loan before year 5, favor the ARM.  \n> If you may keep the loan 7–10+ years and need certainty, favor the fixed."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":4246,"output":1881,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":6127,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1781214477729}}
